#lang ivy1.8

# ############################################################################
#
#   TOMASULO'S ALGORITHM -- liveness
#
#   Companion to `tomasulo_documented.ivy`, which documents the machine and
#   proves its safety (refinement to an in-order reference CPU).  Read that
#   file first: everything about Tomasulo's algorithm, the tag/renaming
#   discipline, the ghost `aux` state and the EPR encoding tricks is explained
#   there and is not repeated here.
#
#   This file proves two LIVENESS properties:
#
#     [live_ready]      every instruction whose operands are already available
#                       eventually retires.  No assumptions beyond weak
#                       fairness of the three arbiters.
#
#     [live_nostarve]   *every* instruction in a reservation station
#                       eventually retires -- no instruction starves.  Proved
#                       under one extra, explicitly declared assumption which
#                       section 5 shows is a genuine expressiveness limit of
#                       EPR rather than a gap in the argument.
#
#   Verified with:
#
#       $ ~/workplace/venv_ivy/bin/ivy_check tomasulo_liveness.ivy
#       ...
#       OK
#
#   The proof technique is McMillan's relational ranking method (CAV 2024) as
#   realised by Ivy's `ranking` tactic; see LIVENESS_GUIDE.md in this
#   directory, and `german_3channels_lex.ivy` for the closest worked example.
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   1.  WHY THE MODEL HAD TO CHANGE
#   ===============================
#
#   `tomasulo_documented.ivy` models one synchronous clock cycle as a single
#   atomic action `toma.step` whose parameters are the arbitration decisions
#   (`issue_choice`, `issue_eu`, `complete_eu`, `st_choice`).  Modelling
#   arbiters as adversarial inputs is exactly right for safety -- it proves
#   correctness for every arbitration policy -- and exactly wrong for
#   liveness, because an adversarial arbiter starves everything.  Worse, a
#   fairness assumption cannot even be *stated* about a monolithic step: one
#   `step` simultaneously retires, dispatches, issues and executes, so a flag
#   saying "this step ran" says nothing about which of those actually made
#   progress, and the dispatch half of the same step can undo the ranking
#   progress made by the issue half.
#
#   So the machine is re-expressed here as a set of guarded rules, one per
#   architectural event, in the style of the other files in this directory
#   (`german_3channels_*.ivy`).  The state is identical; the transitions are
#   the same transitions, decomposed and made asynchronous:
#
#       dispatch(t,srca,srcb,dst)   allocate station t, capture operands,
#                                   rename dst  (environment: not fair)
#       write_reg(r,d) / read_reg(r)  the machine's I/O   (environment)
#       issue(t)                    t's operands are latched into its
#                                   functional unit          [fair: wf_issue]
#       exec_finish(t)              t's functional unit finishes computing
#                                                            [fair: wf_exec]
#       writeback(t)                t wins the common data bus, broadcasts
#                                   (tag t, value), wakes every waiter, and
#                                   frees the station        [fair: wf_cdb]
#
#   Two further differences from the synchronous model, both forced by
#   liveness and both noted in section 9 of the safety file:
#
#   (a) `eu.ready` was an oracle that is never assigned, i.e. a constant.  A
#       unit whose `ready` bit happened to be false would never complete, and
#       nothing would ever retire.  Here `st_ready(t)` is set by the fair rule
#       `exec_finish(t)`: execution takes an arbitrary but finite time.
#
#   (b) The pool of execution units (`eu_t`) is gone; each reservation station
#       owns its functional unit (`st_issued`, `st_ready`, `st_res`).  This is
#       a real design point -- a reservation station with its own attached
#       unit -- and it removes the structural hazard on execution units.  It
#       is a deliberate simplification: with a shared pool, `issue(t)` also
#       needs a free unit, its enabling guard is no longer stable (another
#       station can take the last free unit), and weak fairness of the issue
#       arbiter is then *not* enough -- exactly the situation of
#       `german_3channels_original.ivy`, which needs strong fairness
#       (compassion) for the home's request arbiter.  The single genuinely
#       shared, arbitrated resource that remains here is the one that matters
#       for Tomasulo: the COMMON DATA BUS.  `writeback(t)` is the bus grant,
#       and `globally eventually wf_cdb(T)` is the statement that the bus
#       arbiter is fair.
#
#   Nothing is lost on the safety side: the same refinement invariants are
#   carried over below and re-proved, including the observable statement
#   `read_reg` returns the in-order value (the `ensure` in `read_reg`).
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   2.  THE FAIRNESS INSTRUMENTATION
#   ================================
#
#   Each of the three machine rules gets a boolean flag, parameterised by the
#   station it concerns, pulsed `:= true; := false;` in one atomic step at the
#   TOP of the rule, before the guard is tested.  So
#
#       forall T. globally eventually wf_issue(T)
#
#   says "the issue arbiter offers station T a turn infinitely often", which
#   is an assumption about the SCHEDULER, not about the protocol: it can
#   always be satisfied (when the guard is false the turn is a stutter step),
#   so the assumption is not vacuous.  This is the `wf_*` convention of
#   `german_3channels_lex.ivy`.  The invariants `~wf_issue(T)` etc. record
#   that the flags are only momentarily true, which the l2s monitor needs.
#
#   `dispatch`, `read_reg` and `write_reg` get NO fairness: we never need
#   instructions to arrive.  Liveness here is a statement about instructions
#   that are already in flight.
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   3.  THE GHOST PROGRAM ORDER
#   ===========================
#
#   Liveness of Tomasulo is fundamentally an argument about the WAKE-UP GRAPH:
#   a station waits for the tags in its operand slots, those stations wait for
#   their own tags, and so on.  The argument only works because that graph is
#   acyclic -- a station only ever waits for a station that was dispatched
#   EARLIER.  The model therefore carries a ghost timestamp:
#
#       clock       a logical clock, ticked once per dispatch
#       st_ts(T)    the value of the clock when station T was allocated
#
#   and the fact that makes everything work is [inv_opra_ts] / [inv_oprb_ts]:
#
#       a station waiting on tag P is waiting on a LIVE station that is
#       STRICTLY OLDER than itself.
#
#   The second ghost variable is the witness the ranking needs:
#
#       oldest      a tag naming a live station of minimal timestamp
#
#   maintained by `update_oldest` after every change to the set of live
#   stations.  It is computed with Ivy's `if some ... minimizing ...` form:
#
#       if some m:tag_t. st_valid(m) minimizing st_ts(m) { oldest := m }
#
#   which compiles to "assume m is live and no live station has a smaller
#   timestamp" in the taken branch and "assume no station is live" in the
#   else branch.  That is precisely how one gets "a finite non-empty set has a
#   least element" into a first-order proof -- the fact is not derivable in
#   EPR, and the construct is the sanctioned way to import it (the same device
#   is used by `german_3channels_fifo.ivy`, where the home serves the oldest
#   request).  It buys the invariant [inv_oldest] for free.
#
# ############################################################################

include order
instance ts : unbounded_sequence

# `finite type` is what makes the relational rankings below finite (Rule 5 of
# the paper): `work_created = true` is a legitimate finite bound because the
# whole universe of tags is finite.  Physically: a machine has finitely many
# reservation stations and finitely many architectural registers.
finite type tag_t
finite type reg_t
type word_t

# The ALU, as a relation plus a functionality axiom -- see section 3 of
# `tomasulo_documented.ivy` for why it must not be a function.
relation f(X:word_t,Y:word_t,Z:word_t)
axiom [alu_is_a_function] f(X,Y,Z1) & f(X,Y,Z2) -> Z1 = Z2

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reservation stations (indexed by tag)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
relation st_valid(T:tag_t)          # holds an instruction that has not retired
relation st_issued(T:tag_t)         # operands latched, functional unit started
relation st_ready(T:tag_t)          # the functional unit has finished
var      st_res(T:tag_t) : word_t   # the computed result, waiting for the bus

relation opra_valid(T:tag_t)        # operand A is a value, not a promise
relation opra_tag(T:tag_t,P:tag_t)  # ... otherwise, whose result it waits for
var      opra_val(T:tag_t) : word_t
relation oprb_valid(T:tag_t)
relation oprb_tag(T:tag_t,P:tag_t)
var      oprb_val(T:tag_t) : word_t

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Architectural register file, with renaming information
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
relation ir_resvd(R:reg_t)
relation ir_tag(R:reg_t,T:tag_t)
var      ir_val(R:reg_t) : word_t

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ghost state
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var ref_r(R:reg_t) : word_t         # the in-order reference register file
var aux_opra(T:tag_t) : word_t      # what the in-order machine read ...
var aux_oprb(T:tag_t) : word_t
var aux_res(T:tag_t) : word_t       # ... and computed, for this station
var st_ts(T:tag_t) : ts             # program-order timestamp (section 3)
var clock : ts
var oldest : tag_t                  # a live station of least timestamp

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fairness flags (section 2)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var wf_issue(T:tag_t) : bool
var wf_exec(T:tag_t) : bool
var wf_cdb(T:tag_t) : bool

after init {
    st_valid(T) := false;
    st_issued(T) := false;
    ir_resvd(R) := false;
    # As in the safety file: start the tag relations full rather than
    # unconstrained, so that no initial oracle value can silently block a
    # rule.  No invariant mentions them while nothing is reserved.
    ir_tag(R,T) := true;
    opra_tag(T,P) := true;
    oprb_tag(T,P) := true;
    ref_r(R) := ir_val(R);          # the two machines start in agreement
    wf_issue(T) := false;
    wf_exec(T) := false;
    wf_cdb(T) := false;
    clock := 0;
    clock := clock.next;            # timestamp 0 is never handed out, so
    st_ts(T) := 0;                  # st_ts(T) < clock holds from the start
}

# Recompute the `oldest` witness.  Called after every change to `st_valid`.
action update_oldest = {
    if some m:tag_t. st_valid(m) minimizing st_ts(m) {
        oldest := m
    }
}

# ############################################################################
#  4.  THE MACHINE
# ############################################################################

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dispatch an ALU instruction into station t.
#
# `require ~st_valid(t)` is the structural hazard: in the synchronous model
# this was `stallout`; here a stalled dispatch is simply a rule that does not
# fire.  Operands are read from the register file: a value if the source is
# not renamed, otherwise the source's tag (a promise).  Then dst is renamed to
# t.  Note the reads happen before the writes, so `dispatch(t,r,r,r)` behaves
# like the hardware.
#
# The ghost half runs the in-order reference machine for this instruction and
# records what it read and computed in `aux`.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
action dispatch(t:tag_t, srca:reg_t, srcb:reg_t, dst:reg_t) = {
    require ~st_valid(t);
    var ra : word_t;
    var rb : word_t;
    var rs : word_t;
    ra := ref_r(srca);
    rb := ref_r(srcb);
    rs := *;
    assume f(ra,rb,rs);
    aux_opra(t) := ra;
    aux_oprb(t) := rb;
    aux_res(t)  := rs;

    opra_valid(t) := ~ir_resvd(srca);
    opra_tag(t,X) := ir_tag(srca,X);
    opra_val(t)   := ir_val(srca);
    oprb_valid(t) := ~ir_resvd(srcb);
    oprb_tag(t,X) := ir_tag(srcb,X);
    oprb_val(t)   := ir_val(srcb);

    st_valid(t)  := true;
    st_issued(t) := false;
    st_ready(t)  := false;

    ir_resvd(dst) := true;
    ir_tag(dst,X) := X = t;
    ref_r(dst) := rs;

    st_ts(t) := clock;              # ghost: program order
    clock := clock.next;
    update_oldest;
}

# Write an immediate into a register.  Cancels any outstanding renaming of
# that register -- legal, because from the in-order machine's point of view
# this is the newest writer.
action write_reg(r:reg_t, d:word_t) = {
    ir_val(r)   := d;
    ir_resvd(r) := false;
    ref_r(r)    := d;
}

# The machine's observable output.  A read of a renamed register stalls
# (`require ~ir_resvd(r)`); a read that is answered returns the in-order
# value.  That `ensure` is the refinement theorem, discharged by [inv_reg_ready].
action read_reg(r:reg_t) returns (v:word_t) = {
    require ~ir_resvd(r);
    v := ir_val(r);
    ensure v = ref_r(r)
}

# Issue: both operands are values, so latch them and start the unit.
action issue(t:tag_t) = {
    wf_issue(t) := true;
    wf_issue(t) := false;
    if st_valid(t) & opra_valid(t) & oprb_valid(t) & ~st_issued(t) {
        var v : word_t;
        v := *;
        assume f(opra_val(t),oprb_val(t),v);
        st_res(t)    := v;
        st_issued(t) := true;
        st_ready(t)  := false;
    }
}

# Execution completes, after an arbitrary but finite time.
action exec_finish(t:tag_t) = {
    wf_exec(t) := true;
    wf_exec(t) := false;
    if st_valid(t) & st_issued(t) & ~st_ready(t) {
        st_ready(t) := true;
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The common data bus is granted to station t.
#
# If t has a result, it is broadcast: every register and every operand slot
# waiting for tag t latches the value in this one atomic step (the snooping
# comparator arrays of the RTL, one line of Ivy each), and t is freed.  This
# is the only rule that makes a waiting operand valid, and the only rule that
# frees a station -- which is why it is the top of the ranking hierarchy.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
action writeback(t:tag_t) = {
    wf_cdb(t) := true;
    wf_cdb(t) := false;
    if st_valid(t) & st_issued(t) & st_ready(t) {
        ir_val(R)   := st_res(t) if ir_resvd(R) & ir_tag(R,t) else ir_val(R);
        ir_resvd(R) := ~ir_tag(R,t) & ir_resvd(R);
        opra_val(K)   := st_res(t) if ~opra_valid(K) & opra_tag(K,t) else opra_val(K);
        opra_valid(K) := opra_tag(K,t) | opra_valid(K);
        oprb_val(K)   := st_res(t) if ~oprb_valid(K) & oprb_tag(K,t) else oprb_val(K);
        oprb_valid(K) := oprb_tag(K,t) | oprb_valid(K);
        st_valid(t) := false;
        update_oldest;
    }
}

export dispatch
export write_reg
export read_reg
export issue
export exec_finish
export writeback

# ############################################################################
#  5.  INVARIANTS
# ############################################################################

# --- the fairness flags are only momentarily true ---------------------------
invariant ~wf_issue(T)
invariant ~wf_exec(T)
invariant ~wf_cdb(T)

# --- the refinement invariants, carried over from tomasulo_documented.ivy ---
# (their meaning is explained in section 7 of that file)
invariant [inv_opra_val]     st_valid(K) & opra_valid(K) -> opra_val(K) = aux_opra(K)
invariant [inv_oprb_val]     st_valid(K) & oprb_valid(K) -> oprb_val(K) = aux_oprb(K)
invariant [inv_reg_ready]    ~ir_resvd(R) -> ir_val(R) = ref_r(R)
invariant [inv_reg_reserved] ir_resvd(R) & ir_tag(R,K) -> st_valid(K) & aux_res(K) = ref_r(R)
invariant [inv_opra_dep]     st_valid(K) & ~opra_valid(K) & opra_tag(K,T)
                                 -> st_valid(T) & aux_res(T) = aux_opra(K)
invariant [inv_oprb_dep]     st_valid(K) & ~oprb_valid(K) & oprb_tag(K,T)
                                 -> st_valid(T) & aux_res(T) = aux_oprb(K)
invariant [inv_aux_alu]      st_valid(T) -> f(aux_opra(T),aux_oprb(T),aux_res(T))
# the value sitting in a started functional unit is the in-order result
invariant [inv_res]          st_valid(T) & st_issued(T) -> st_res(T) = aux_res(T)

# --- the program-order invariants, new here ---------------------------------
invariant [inv_ts_clock]  st_valid(T) -> st_ts(T) < clock
invariant [inv_reg_ts]    ir_resvd(R) & ir_tag(R,K) -> st_ts(K) < clock

# THE WAKE-UP GRAPH IS ACYCLIC.  A station only ever waits for a strictly
# older station.  Inductive because at dispatch the new station gets the
# largest timestamp, and the tags it captures come from the register file,
# where [inv_reg_ts] says every renaming is older than the clock.
invariant [inv_opra_ts]   st_valid(K) & ~opra_valid(K) & opra_tag(K,T) -> st_ts(T) < st_ts(K)
invariant [inv_oprb_ts]   st_valid(K) & ~oprb_valid(K) & oprb_tag(K,T) -> st_ts(T) < st_ts(K)

# `oldest` really is a live station of least timestamp.  Free, from the
# `minimizing` form in `update_oldest` (section 3).
invariant [inv_oldest]    st_valid(M) -> st_valid(oldest) & st_ts(oldest) <= st_ts(M)

# ############################################################################
#  6.  FAIRNESS ASSUMPTIONS
# ############################################################################
#
# One per arbiter, all weak (justice).  No compassion anywhere -- see
# section 1 (b) for the design choice that makes weak fairness sufficient.

explicit temporal axiom [wfa_issue] forall T. globally eventually wf_issue(T)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_exec]  forall T. globally eventually wf_exec(T)
explicit temporal axiom [wfa_cdb]   forall T. globally eventually wf_cdb(T)

# ############################################################################
#  7.  FIRST PROPERTY: an instruction with its operands retires
# ############################################################################
#
#       forall T. globally ( st_valid(T) & opra_valid(T) & oprb_valid(T)
#                            -> eventually ~st_valid(T) )
#
# "Once an instruction's operands are available, it retires."  This is the
# statement that the back end -- issue, execute, writeback -- never deadlocks
# and never starves anybody, and it needs no assumption beyond the three weak
# fairness conditions.  It is proved BEFORE the extra assumption of section 8
# is declared, so it does not depend on it.
#
# THE RANKING.  Station _T walks a three-stage pipeline
#
#       ~issued   --issue(_T)-->   issued & ~ready
#                 --exec_finish(_T)-->   issued & ready
#                 --writeback(_T)-->   retired
#
# so this is the textbook Rule-8/Rule-10 pipeline of LIVENESS_GUIDE section 8,
# with one component per stage.  Following the recipe there, each lower-order
# component ranks on the UNION of the stages still to come, so that the action
# advancing _T one stage conserves it:
#
#   [00]  {_T}                      reduced by writeback  (highest order)
#   [01]  {_T} while not (issued and ready)   reduced by exec_finish
#   [02]  {_T} while not issued              reduced by issue
#
# `issue(_T)` sets `st_issued` and clears `st_ready`, so _T stays inside [01];
# it only leaves [01] when `exec_finish` fires.  Each scheduler is literally
# the enabling guard of its rule, which is what `l2s_progress` needs: unlike
# conservation and stability, progress is not discounted by preemption, so a
# component must reduce its own ranking whenever its scheduler is on.
#
# `l2s_sched_exists` (premise S4) is the case split "_T is not issued, or is
# issued and not ready, or is issued and ready" -- exhaustive, so something is
# always scheduled.  That is the whole content of the proof.

explicit temporal property [live_ready]
  forall T. globally ((st_valid(T) & opra_valid(T) & oprb_valid(T))
                      -> eventually ~st_valid(T))
proof {
  tactic skolemizenp;
  instantiate wfa_issue;
  instantiate wfa_exec;
  instantiate wfa_cdb;
  tactic ranking with {
    # [00] the bus grant retires _T.
    definition work_created[00](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[00](M:tag_t)  = M = _T & st_valid(_T)
    definition work_invar[00]            = st_valid(_T) & opra_valid(_T) & oprb_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[00](N:tag_t)= wf_cdb(N)
    definition work_helpful[00](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = _T & M = _T
                                           & st_valid(_T) & st_issued(_T) & st_ready(_T)

    # [01] execution completes.  The ranking is the union of the two stages
    # still to come, so `issue` conserves it.
    definition work_created[01](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[01](M:tag_t)  = M = _T & st_valid(_T)
                                           & ~(st_issued(_T) & st_ready(_T))
    definition work_invar[01]            = st_valid(_T) & opra_valid(_T) & oprb_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[01](N:tag_t)= wf_exec(N)
    definition work_helpful[01](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = _T & M = _T
                                           & st_valid(_T) & st_issued(_T) & ~st_ready(_T)

    # [02] the instruction is issued into its functional unit.
    definition work_created[02](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[02](M:tag_t)  = M = _T & st_valid(_T) & ~st_issued(_T)
    definition work_invar[02]            = st_valid(_T) & opra_valid(_T) & oprb_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[02](N:tag_t)= wf_issue(N)
    definition work_helpful[02](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = _T & M = _T
                                           & st_valid(_T) & ~st_issued(_T)
  }
}

# ############################################################################
#  8.  THE ONE EXTRA ASSUMPTION
# ############################################################################
#
#   "A station that is waiting for an operand is waiting for SOMETHING."
#
#   This is the only thing below that is assumed rather than proved, and it is
#   worth being precise about what it is and why it is here, because it is a
#   textbook instance of the stratification pitfall of LIVENESS_GUIDE section
#   4.5 / paper section 4.2.
#
#   WHAT THE PROOF NEEDS.  Premise S4 of Rule 10 (`l2s_sched_exists`) says
#   that while the liveness invariant holds, some component is scheduled.  For
#   [live_nostarve] the liveness invariant is "_T is still in its station",
#   and the state that would falsify S4 is "every live station at least as old
#   as _T is blocked waiting for an operand" -- a deadlocked wake-up graph.
#   The machine cannot reach such a state, and the argument is one line:
#
#       `oldest` is a live station of least timestamp ([inv_oldest]); if it
#       were waiting for a tag P then P would be live and strictly older
#       ([inv_opra_dep], [inv_opra_ts]), contradicting minimality.
#
#   Notice this needs NO induction over the chain and NO pigeonhole argument:
#   one step from the minimum suffices.  That is exactly what the `minimizing`
#   witness in `update_oldest` was introduced for.
#
#   WHAT EPR CANNOT SAY.  The argument silently uses "if `oldest` is waiting
#   then there IS some tag P it waits for", i.e.
#
#       forall K. st_valid(K) & ~opra_valid(K) -> exists P. opra_tag(K,P)
#
#   a universally quantified tag in the scope of an existentially quantified
#   tag.  That is a self-loop in the quantifier-alternation graph, and
#   `ivy_check` rejects it outright:
#
#       error: The verification condition is not in the fragment FAU.
#       The following terms may generate an infinite sequence of instantiations:
#         otag(K,T_a)   (position 1 is a function from tag_t to tag_t)
#         skolem function defined by: exists T_a. otag(K,T_a)
#
#   Every repair one would try fails the same way, because they are all the
#   same formula: storing the producer as a scalar `opra_tagv(K) : tag_t`
#   makes the invariant `opra_tag(K,opra_tagv(K))`, rejected with the same
#   message; recording the producer's timestamp `opra_pts(K) : ts` moves the
#   existential to "some live station has timestamp W", which needs an arc
#   ts -> tag_t and closes a cycle with `st_ts : tag_t -> ts`.  The fact is
#   simply "this relation is non-empty", and non-emptiness of a relation is
#   not expressible in EPR at all.
#
#   CONTRAST WITH THE TICKET PROTOCOL.  `ticket_ranking.ivy` in this directory
#   gets away with the structurally identical invariant
#
#       forall K:ticket. next_ticket > K & service <= K
#                        -> exists T:thread. m(T,K) & ~pc1(T)
#
#   precisely because the quantifiers are over DIFFERENT sorts and the map
#   thread -> ticket is kept as a relation `m(T,K)` -- the file's own comment
#   says "use relation and not a function to be in EPR".  The arc
#   ticket -> thread is then acyclic.  Tomasulo has no such luck: a station
#   waits for a station, so the arc is tag_t -> tag_t whatever one does.
#
#   THE ASSUMPTION.  We therefore assume the statement only at the ground term
#   `oldest`, where the existential Skolemises to a CONSTANT and no cycle
#   arises.  It is declared as a non-explicit `temporal axiom` of the form
#   `globally P`, which Ivy adds to the model as an assumed invariant.  It is
#   the weakest form of the missing fact: it says nothing about who the
#   producer is, only that a waiting slot is waiting for somebody, and
#   everything else -- that the producer is live, that it is older, that this
#   contradicts minimality -- is proved from [inv_opra_dep], [inv_opra_ts] and
#   [inv_oldest].  Section 10 shows that removing it fails exactly one check,
#   `l2s_sched_exists`, and nothing else.
#
#   That the assumption is TRUE of the machine is immediate by inspection of
#   the code: `opra_tag(t,X) := ir_tag(srca,X)` is the only assignment to an
#   operand tag relation, `ir_tag(dst,X) := X = t` and the full initialisation
#   `ir_tag(R,T) := true` are the only assignments to a register tag relation,
#   and none of the three can produce the empty relation.

temporal axiom [oldest_waits_for_someone]
  globally ((st_valid(oldest) & ~opra_valid(oldest) -> exists P. opra_tag(oldest,P))
          & (st_valid(oldest) & ~oprb_valid(oldest) -> exists P. oprb_tag(oldest,P)))

# ############################################################################
#  9.  SECOND PROPERTY: no instruction starves
# ############################################################################
#
#       forall T. globally ( st_valid(T) -> eventually ~st_valid(T) )
#
# Every instruction that enters a reservation station eventually leaves it.
# Since a station is freed only by the broadcast of its own result, this says
# that every dispatched instruction is eventually executed and written back:
# out-of-order execution does not starve anybody, no matter how the
# dependences are tangled and no matter how many later instructions overtake.
#
# THE RANKING.  Write
#
#       A  =  { M : st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T) }
#
# for the live stations that are at least as old as _T.  A contains _T, and A
# is the top-order ranking:
#
#   [00]  A                                      reduced by writeback
#   [01]  A minus the (issued and ready) stations reduced by exec_finish
#   [02]  A minus the issued stations             reduced by issue
#
# WHY A IS A LEGAL RANKING -- this is the crux, and it is where the ghost
# timestamps earn their keep.  A must never grow:
#
#   * `dispatch(t)` gives t the timestamp `clock`, and [inv_ts_clock] with
#     `st_valid(_T)` (the liveness invariant) gives st_ts(_T) < clock.  So a
#     freshly dispatched station is strictly YOUNGER than _T and lands outside
#     A.  This is the formal content of "later instructions cannot delay an
#     earlier one indefinitely": the machine may keep dispatching forever, and
#     the ranking does not notice.
#   * `writeback` removes a station from A and adds none.
#   * `issue` and `exec_finish` do not touch `st_valid` or `st_ts` at all.
#
# and A is finite because `tag_t` is a finite type, so `work_created = true`
# is a legitimate finite bound (Rule 5).
#
# [01] and [02] are again unions of the remaining stages, for the same reason
# as in section 7, so that `issue` conserves [01] and nothing conserves less
# than it must.
#
# WHY SOMETHING IS ALWAYS SCHEDULED (premise S4).  `oldest` is in A: it is
# live and of minimal timestamp, so certainly no younger than _T.  By the
# argument of section 8 it is not waiting for an operand.  So it is either not
# issued (scheduler [02] fires, and `issue(oldest)`'s guard is exactly
# "live, operands valid, not issued"), or issued and not ready ([01]), or
# issued and ready ([00]).  Exhaustive.
#
# WHY THE ORDER IS LEXICOGRAPHIC RATHER THAN A FLAT RULE-8 PROOF.  [00] must
# preempt [01] and [02]: when a station M other than _T is written back, M
# leaves A, which SHRINKS [01] and [02] as well -- that is fine -- but M's
# broadcast also makes operands valid all over the machine and can move
# stations between the stages that [01] and [02] measure.  Keeping [00]
# highest means the two lower components carry no conservation obligation in
# exactly the states where the bus is active.  In practice the components here
# are conserved anyway; the lexicographic reading is the one that explains why
# the proof is structured this way, and `ranking` is in any case the more
# robust tactic on this build (LIVENESS_GUIDE section 4.4).

explicit temporal property [live_nostarve]
  forall T. globally (st_valid(T) -> eventually ~st_valid(T))
proof {
  tactic skolemizenp;
  instantiate wfa_issue;
  instantiate wfa_exec;
  instantiate wfa_cdb;
  tactic ranking with {
    # [00] the bus retires one of the stations at least as old as _T.
    definition work_created[00](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[00](M:tag_t)  = st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
    definition work_invar[00]            = st_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[00](N:tag_t)= wf_cdb(N)
    definition work_helpful[00](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = M & st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
                                                   & st_issued(M) & st_ready(M)

    # [01] one of them finishes executing.
    definition work_created[01](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[01](M:tag_t)  = st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
                                           & ~(st_issued(M) & st_ready(M))
    definition work_invar[01]            = st_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[01](N:tag_t)= wf_exec(N)
    definition work_helpful[01](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = M & st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
                                                   & st_issued(M) & ~st_ready(M)

    # [02] one of them is issued.  The scheduler carries the operand-readiness
    # conjuncts because they are part of `issue`'s guard: without them
    # `l2s_progress[02]` fails (section 10, ablation C).
    definition work_created[02](M:tag_t) = true
    definition work_needed[02](M:tag_t)  = st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
                                           & ~st_issued(M)
    definition work_invar[02]            = st_valid(_T)
    definition work_progress[02](N:tag_t)= wf_issue(N)
    definition work_helpful[02](N:tag_t,M:tag_t) = N = M & st_valid(M) & st_ts(M) <= st_ts(_T)
                                                   & ~st_issued(M)
                                                   & opra_valid(M) & oprb_valid(M)
  }
}

# ############################################################################
#
#  10.  NON-VACUITY / LOAD-BEARING CHECKS  (all actually run)
#  =========================================================
#
#  On [live_nostarve]:
#
#    A. delete `temporal axiom [oldest_waits_for_someone]`
#         -> 7 failures, all of them the SAME check `l2s_sched_exists` (it is
#            re-checked once per exported action) and nothing else.  The
#            assumption is used for exactly one premise, S4, which is what
#            section 8 claims.
#    B. `work_progress[00] := false`
#         -> `l2s_progress[00]`, `l2s_progress_eventually[00]`,
#            `l2s_sched_stable[00] ... FAIL`.  The bus fairness is what
#            reduces the top ranking.
#    C. drop `opra_valid(M) & oprb_valid(M)` from `work_helpful[02]`
#         -> `l2s_progress[02] ... FAIL`.  The scheduler must be the enabling
#            guard of `issue`: scheduling a blocked station does not make
#            progress.  (This is the check that shows the stable scheduler is
#            doing real work and not decoration.)
#    D. delete component [00] entirely
#         -> `l2s_sched_exists ... FAIL` again (and only that).  Two
#            components cannot cover the "issued and ready" state.
#    E. do not `instantiate wfa_cdb`
#         -> `l2s_progress[00]`, `l2s_progress_eventually[00] ... FAIL`.
#
#  On the model itself, in the same spirit as section 8 of the safety file:
#  adding `invariant ~(st_valid(T) & st_issued(T))` makes `ivy_check` report
#  exactly one failure, in `issue`, so instructions really are dispatched and
#  issued and the rules are not all vacuously blocked by their `assume`s.
#
#  11.  WHAT IS STILL OPEN
#  =======================
#
#  * The shared execution-unit pool of `tomasulo_documented.ivy` (section
#    1 (b)).  With a pool, `issue`'s guard "some unit is free" is not stable,
#    so weak fairness of the issue arbiter is not enough; the honest statement
#    would add a compassion assumption
#
#      forall T. (globally eventually (T is issuable & some unit is free))
#                -> (globally eventually T is issued)
#
#    and discharge its antecedent from "every busy unit is eventually freed",
#    in the three-way tableau case split of `german_3channels_original.ivy` /
#    `strongfair.ivy`.  That is the natural next file.
#
#  * Removing the assumption of section 8.  It is not a matter of finding a
#    better ranking: the missing fact is the non-emptiness of a tag-to-tag
#    relation, which EPR cannot state.  Two escapes are conceivable, neither
#    of them free: pay for liveness in the model instead of in the assumption,
#    as `german_3channels_fifo.ivy` does (e.g. dispatch in a fixed circular
#    order so that the oldest live station is a maintained pointer rather than
#    a minimum -- but the circular successor is again a tag_t -> tag_t
#    function); or step outside the decidable fragment for that one
#    obligation and discharge it with an interactive proof.
#
# ############################################################################
